Pointers (Secondary / Derived Data Types)

 What is a Pointer?
A pointer is one of the secondary (derived) data types. It is used to store the address of any other variable. Using a pointer, we can access data indirectly.

Syntax:

datatype *var_name;


Examples:

int *p;   // p is an integer pointer -> p is a variable of type "pointer to int"
char *q;  // q is a character pointer -> q is a variable of type "pointer to char"


 Wild Pointer

int i, j;
int *p;   // wild pointer

When a pointer is pointing to an unknown/garbage location (address), it is called a wild pointer.



 Direct vs Indirect Access

int main() {
    int i, j;
    int *p;

    i = 10, j = 20;
    p = &i;

    // printf("&i: %u\n", &i);
    // printf("p: %u\n", p);

    printf("dir: %d\n", i);     // direct   -> gives value
    printf("indir: %d\n", *p);  // indirect -> gives value

    return 0;
}


Format specifiers:
- %u → gives the decimal value (also gives a warning when used with a pointer)
- %p → gives the hexadecimal value (address)



 Modifying a Variable: Directly vs Indirectly

int main() {
    int i;
    int *p;

    i = 100;
    p = &i;
    printf("i=%d  *p=%d\n", i, *p);   // i=100  *p=100

    i = 150;                          // modify directly
    printf("i=%d  *p=%d\n", i, *p);   // i=150  *p=150

    *p = 200;                         // modify indirectly (through pointer)
    printf("i=%d  *p=%d\n", i, *p);   // i=200  *p=200

    return 0;
}




 sizeof() a Pointer

int main() {
    int i;
    char ch;
    float f;
    double d;

    int    *ip = &i;
    char   *cp = &ch;
    float  *fp = &f;
    double *dp = &d;

    printf("%ld\n", sizeof(ip));  // 8
    printf("%ld\n", sizeof(cp));  // 8
    printf("%ld\n", sizeof(fp));  // 8
    printf("%ld\n", sizeof(dp));  // 8

    return 0;
}


> Size of a pointer is always 8 bytes (on a 64-bit system), regardless of the data type it points to — because it only needs to store a memory address, not the value itself.
